  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Solar landscape lighting is the first thing most homeowners try. The stakes cost a few dollars each, there is no wiring, and the box promises dusk-to-dawn light with zero electricity. Then October arrives, the cedar elms are still leafed out, and the path lights fade by 10 p.m. If you are deciding between solar landscape lighting and a wired 12V low-voltage LED system for an Austin property, this guide lays out the real trade-offs. Austin Landscape Lighting installs low-voltage systems for a living, but we also tell clients when a solar fixture is the sensible choice. Both have a place, and the right answer depends on sun exposure, what you need the light to do, and how long you want it to last.</p>

<h2>How Each System Actually Works</h2>
<p>A solar landscape light is a self-contained unit. A small photovoltaic panel charges a battery during the day, a photocell switches the LED on at dusk, and the battery runs the light until it drains or the sun comes up. Every fixture is independent, which is why installation is nothing more than pushing a stake into the ground.</p>
<p>A low-voltage system starts at a transformer plugged into a GFCI outlet. The transformer steps household 120V down to 12V, and direct-burial cable carries that power to each fixture. The transformer handles timing through a photocell, an astronomical timer, or an app-connected controller. Fixtures share power, so brightness is consistent and the system runs as long as you tell it to.</p>
<h2>Brightness and Beam Control</h2>
<p>Most retail solar path lights produce 5 to 20 lumens. A typical low-voltage LED path light delivers 100 to 300 lumens, and an uplight for a tree can run 400 to 900 lumens. That is not a small gap. Solar output is enough to mark the edge of a walkway in open sky. It is not enough to light the trunk of a 40-foot live oak, wash a limestone wall, or make a flagstone step safe to use.</p>
<p>Beam control is the second issue. Quality low-voltage fixtures accept interchangeable lamps in 15, 30, 45, and 60 degree spreads, so a designer can put a narrow 15 degree beam up a tall cedar elm trunk and a wide 60 degree wash across an Austin stone facade. Solar fixtures almost always use a fixed, omnidirectional LED with no optics. You get a small puddle of light around the stake and little ability to aim it.</p>
<p>Color temperature also varies. Many solar fixtures ship at 5000K to 6500K, a bluish white that looks harsh against warm stone and foliage. Low-voltage LED lamps are standard at 2700K warm white, which flatters limestone, cedar, and brick. Some premium solar models now offer 2700K to 3000K, so check the spec sheet if you go that route.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/landscaping-solar-lighting-ideas-2.webp" alt="Row of solar stake lights along a sunny garden bed with no overhead tree cover" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>Solar fixtures perform best in open beds with six or more hours of direct sun, which is rarer than it sounds in tree-covered Austin neighborhoods.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>The Shade Problem in Austin Yards</h2>
<p>This is where solar landscape lighting most often disappoints local homeowners. A solar panel needs roughly six to eight hours of direct sun to fully charge a battery. Walk through Tarrytown, Allandale, Westlake, or Travis Heights and look up. Mature live oaks hold their leaves year-round, and their canopies can cover an entire front yard. Cedar elms, pecans, and Ashe junipers add more cover. A path light sitting in dappled shade may collect two or three hours of weak charge, enough for a dim glow that quits early.</p>
<p>Winter compounds the issue. Shorter days mean less charging time, yet the fixture needs to run longer because it gets dark by 5:45 p.m. Many solar lights that seemed fine in June are barely visible by December. A string of cloudy days in January can leave them dark altogether.</p>
<p>Summer brings a different stressor. Austin regularly runs 100 degrees or more for weeks. Heat accelerates battery degradation, and the small NiMH or lithium cells in solar stakes typically lose meaningful capacity within one to two years. Replacement batteries cost nearly as much as a new fixture, so most people just replace the light.</p>

<h2>Lifespan and Maintenance</h2>
<p>A well-built brass or copper low-voltage fixture should last 15 to 25 years. The housing develops a patina but does not corrode, and when the LED lamp eventually dims after 40,000 hours or so, you swap a lamp rather than the fixture. Cable buried 6 inches and connected with waterproof gel-filled connectors rarely fails.</p>
<p>Solar fixtures are mostly plastic or thin aluminum. Panels cloud and scratch, stakes snap when a mower bumps them, and the battery is the weak link. Realistic lifespan for a budget solar light in Central Texas sun is one to three years. Premium solar fixtures with replaceable lithium batteries and glass panels can stretch that to five years or more, but they cost 60 to 150 dollars each, which closes much of the price gap with wired fixtures.</p>
<p>Deer are a factor in Northwest Austin, Steiner Ranch, and the Hill Country edges. A deer stepping on a plastic solar stake ends it. Brass fixtures on 10-inch ground stakes survive the same encounter.</p>

<h2>Cost Comparison Over Ten Years</h2>
<p>Upfront cost is where solar wins decisively. The following table compares planning ranges for a typical front-yard project with eight path lights and four uplights. These are planning ranges based on local market pricing, not quotes.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Retail solar fixtures</th><th>Low-voltage LED system</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Upfront cost (12 fixtures)</td><td>150 to 600 dollars</td><td>2,400 to 5,500 dollars installed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical output per path light</td><td>5 to 20 lumens</td><td>100 to 300 lumens</td></tr>
<tr><td>Beam control</td><td>Fixed, omnidirectional</td><td>15 to 60 degree interchangeable</td></tr>
