<p>Over a decade, solar still costs less in raw dollars, but the gap narrows considerably once you count replacement sets. More importantly, the two columns do not buy the same thing. One marks a path. The other lights a property. You can run your own numbers for a wired system with our <a href="/tools/cost-estimator/">cost estimator</a>.</p>

<h2>When Solar Landscape Lighting Makes Sense</h2>
<p>Solar is not a bad product. It is a product with a narrow sweet spot, and inside that sweet spot it is the right call.</p>
<h3>Remote spots with no power</h3>
<p>A back fence line 200 feet from the house, a mailbox at the end of a long Hill Country driveway, or a garden shed on an acreage lot may be impractical to wire. Voltage drop on a 12V run that long requires heavy 10-gauge cable or a second transformer, and trenching that far adds cost. A couple of premium solar fixtures with good sun exposure solve the problem for a fraction of the price.</p>
<h3>Full-sun beds and open lots</h3>
<p>New-construction neighborhoods in Pflugerville, Manor, Kyle, and Leander often have young trees and open sky. Solar fixtures charge fully there and can perform reasonably for several years. Once the trees mature, you may want to revisit the plan.</p>
<h3>Rentals and temporary needs</h3>
<p>If you are renting, staging a home for sale, or lighting a seasonal display, a wired system is more than you need. Solar lets you add and remove fixtures with no commitment.</p>
<h3>Decorative accents, not task lighting</h3>
<p>Solar string lights over a patio or small solar lanterns on a garden table add ambiance. They are not expected to make steps safe or deter anyone, so their low output is not a flaw.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/low-voltage-landscape-lighting-austin-1.webp" alt="Brass low-voltage uplights illuminating live oak trunks and a limestone facade on an Austin home" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>A 12V low-voltage system delivers enough output to uplight mature live oaks and graze an Austin stone facade, tasks no solar fixture can handle.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>When Low-Voltage LED Is the Right Investment</h2>
<p>If you want the property to look designed after dark rather than dotted with small glowing stakes, wired low-voltage is the only realistic path. A few specific situations make the case clearly.</p>
<h3>Tree and architectural lighting</h3>
<p>Uplighting a live oak canopy takes a 400 to 900 lumen fixture with a 30 to 45 degree beam, aimed carefully and shielded so the light stays on the tree. Grazing Austin stone with a fixture set 8 to 12 inches from the wall brings out every shadow in the texture. Both require real output and real optics. Our <a href="/services/garden-and-tree-lighting/">garden and tree lighting</a> service is built around these techniques.</p>
<h3>Safety on steps and slopes</h3>
<p>Hill Country lots in Westlake, Barton Creek, and Lakeway come with grade changes. Steps and retaining walls need consistent, reliable light every night of the year. A path light staggered every 6 to 8 feet along a walkway and a recessed step light under each tread cannot depend on yesterday's weather.</p>
<h3>Control and scheduling</h3>
<p>A low-voltage transformer with an astronomical timer turns on at sunset and off at a time you choose, every day, adjusting automatically through the seasons. An app-connected controller adds zones, dimming, and scenes, so the patio can run late while the front yard shuts down at 11 p.m. Solar fixtures offer none of this, which also matters for dark-sky friendly operation and for HOAs that ask for lights off after a set hour.</p>
<h3>Resale value and curb appeal</h3>
<p>Buyers and appraisers notice a professionally lit facade. A set of plastic solar stakes along the walk does not register the same way, and a listing agent may ask you to pull them.</p>

<h2>A Hybrid Approach That Works</h2>
<p>Plenty of Austin Landscape Lighting clients end up with both systems. The wired low-voltage system handles the house, the trees, the paths, and the patio. Premium solar fixtures mark the far corners of the lot, the back gate, or a sunny raised bed near the alley where a cable run makes no sense. The key is choosing solar fixtures that match the wired system in color temperature so the property reads as one design rather than two.</p>
<p>If you are ready to upgrade, a <a href="/services/low-voltage-landscape-lighting/">low-voltage landscape lighting</a> installation reuses nothing from the solar set, so there is no sunk cost beyond the fixtures themselves.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/low-voltage-landscape-lighting-austin-3.webp" alt="Low-voltage transformer mounted beside a garage with cable runs feeding path and accent lights" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="900"><figcaption>A single transformer sized with 20 percent headroom can power dozens of LED fixtures across multiple zones with consistent brightness.</figcaption></figure>

